00:06:00.910 --> 00:06:03.368
FRANCES PERRY: OK, so I'm a
Tech Lead on the Cloud Dataflow

00:06:03.368 --> 00:06:05.250
project, where I
focus particularly

00:06:05.250 --> 00:06:07.410
on the user-facing
surface of the project.

00:06:07.410 --> 00:06:12.780
So that's our client-side
SDKs, monitoring UIs, tooling,

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:14.670
documentation, and
basically making

00:06:14.670 --> 00:06:16.470
sure that at the Cloud
Dataflow service,

00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:19.470
we give users a coherent vision
for how they're executing

00:06:19.470 --> 00:06:20.610
their processing pipelines.

00:06:20.610 --> 00:06:21.360
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:25.210
All right, so, at a high
level, what is Dataflow?

00:06:25.210 --> 00:06:27.315
What is this thing, and
what does it get used for?

00:06:27.315 --> 00:06:29.220
FRANCES PERRY: Yeah,
so Cloud Dataflow

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:30.960
is a fully managed
service that's

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:34.020
fully integrated with Google
Cloud Platform for executing

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:36.790
batch and streaming data
processing pipelines.

00:06:36.790 --> 00:06:38.820
So you can think of it
sort of as a successor

00:06:38.820 --> 00:06:40.620
to systems like MapReduce.

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:43.620
But it also brings in a lot
of the more recent streaming

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:44.910
technologies.

00:06:44.910 --> 00:06:47.580
So it can do both sort of your
traditional batch ETL-style

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:49.770
things, as well as
real-time applications.

00:06:49.770 --> 00:06:52.440
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool, so
when I think about MapReduce,

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:54.240
I think basically--
so I have big data.

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:55.890
You run a big job.

00:06:55.890 --> 00:06:57.366
And then you're done.

00:06:57.366 --> 00:06:58.740
Like you run it
during the night,

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:01.350
and hopefully the morning
after, you have the results.

00:07:01.350 --> 00:07:03.150
But you're saying
that Dataflow also

00:07:03.150 --> 00:07:04.440
allows you to do streaming.

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:06.360
What kind of use
case have you seen?

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:08.310
Like why would I use Dataflow?

00:07:08.310 --> 00:07:09.976
FRANCES PERRY: So I
think the thing that

00:07:09.976 --> 00:07:12.870
makes Dataflow really unique
is basically two things.

00:07:12.870 --> 00:07:15.450
So there's first of all, the
programming model, or the way

00:07:15.450 --> 00:07:16.920
you write your programs.

00:07:16.920 --> 00:07:18.390
And then there's
also the service,

00:07:18.390 --> 00:07:20.620
which is fully no knobs,
and fully managed.

00:07:20.620 --> 00:07:23.490
So basically, it tries to do as
much of the tuning and things

00:07:23.490 --> 00:07:24.810
as it can for you.

00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:26.280
On the programming
model side, this

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:28.740
is really where we're
tying into Apache Beam.

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:31.650
And so, this programming
model that you use for talk--

00:07:31.650 --> 00:07:34.110
for describing your
data processing

00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:37.740
is really a new way to think
about how to process data

00:07:37.740 --> 00:07:39.850
across distributed systems.

00:07:39.850 --> 00:07:42.600
So if you go back to
2004, what MapReduce

00:07:42.600 --> 00:07:44.970
did when we published
that paper is really

00:07:44.970 --> 00:07:46.710
gave us a new set
of abstractions

00:07:46.710 --> 00:07:49.290
for thinking about what, until
then, had been a extremely

00:07:49.290 --> 00:07:53.220
complex problem about how
to process data in parallel

00:07:53.220 --> 00:07:56.610
across multiple machines
and have fault tolerance

00:07:56.610 --> 00:07:59.410
and be able to do it
efficiently at scale.

00:07:59.410 --> 00:08:02.550
So, we think of basically
the beam model in the way

00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:04.040
that you're writing
these programs

00:08:04.040 --> 00:08:07.110
as doing that next step,
but now for unifying

00:08:07.110 --> 00:08:10.450
both batch processing and your
real-time streaming processing.

00:08:10.450 --> 00:08:12.870
MARK MANDEL: So, what exactly
is real-time streaming?

00:08:12.870 --> 00:08:14.130
What does that mean?

00:08:14.130 --> 00:08:15.580
Like, for a lot of people
who don't necessarily

00:08:15.580 --> 00:08:16.080
deal with that.

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:17.746
FRANCES PERRY: So
this could be anything

00:08:17.746 --> 00:08:20.500
where you're processing, really,
an infinite amount of data.

00:08:20.500 --> 00:08:23.220
So if you think about the way
data is generated these days,

00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:25.270
it's really often in
an infinite stream.

00:08:25.270 --> 00:08:27.150
So I think one of my
favorite examples to use

00:08:27.150 --> 00:08:29.780
is a mobile gaming application.

00:08:29.780 --> 00:08:32.280
So let's say that we've just
launched some new mobile gaming

00:08:32.280 --> 00:08:35.280
application, and we've got
users all around the world

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:36.960
frantically crushing candy--

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:39.480
doing something to earn
points for their team, right?

00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:41.789
And these scores are
coming back to our system.

00:08:41.789 --> 00:08:43.890
And we want to go ahead
and analyze what's

00:08:43.890 --> 00:08:45.910
the top scoring team right now.

00:08:45.910 --> 00:08:48.520
So in a system like that,
once the game launches,

00:08:48.520 --> 00:08:50.775
that data just keeps
coming in, right?

00:08:50.775 --> 00:08:53.160
In a traditional sort of
batch-style processing,

00:08:53.160 --> 00:08:56.380
what you do is chunk
your logs into days.

00:08:56.380 --> 00:08:58.410
So here's my logs
from Monday, and now I

00:08:58.410 --> 00:08:59.490
can analyze them, right?

00:08:59.490 --> 00:09:01.530
Maybe, so, Tuesday
at 1:00 AM, I can

00:09:01.530 --> 00:09:04.690
start analyzing Monday's logs
because I have all that data.

00:09:04.690 --> 00:09:06.910
So that's sort of doing it
in a batch-style system.

00:09:06.910 --> 00:09:08.670
But in a streaming or
a real-time system,

00:09:08.670 --> 00:09:10.740
you want to be doing
things as the data arrives.

00:09:10.740 --> 00:09:12.060
You want much lower latency.

00:09:12.060 --> 00:09:14.310
You want to have a leader
board that you're displaying

00:09:14.310 --> 00:09:16.560
of the current top team, right?

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:19.990
And that becomes-- so both low
latency is important there,

00:09:19.990 --> 00:09:22.680
and also, because of
distributed systems,

00:09:22.680 --> 00:09:25.020
you need the flexibility
to handle data

00:09:25.020 --> 00:09:27.100
that comes in out of order.

00:09:27.100 --> 00:09:29.850
Because if you think about
how distributed systems work,

00:09:29.850 --> 00:09:33.035
you can't guarantee that your
data will come in immediately

00:09:33.035 --> 00:09:33.660
on time, right?

00:09:33.660 --> 00:09:35.550
People's network
connections get delayed,

00:09:35.550 --> 00:09:36.940
phones go offline, and so on.

00:09:36.940 --> 00:09:39.450
So you need to be able
to adjust and process

00:09:39.450 --> 00:09:42.370
these sort of out-of-order
infinite data sets.

00:09:42.370 --> 00:09:45.060
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool, so,
we've been mentioning two

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:46.810
sides of the same thing, right?

00:09:46.810 --> 00:09:50.190
We've been mentioning on one
side, the programming model,

00:09:50.190 --> 00:09:52.480
which is kind of like
MapReduce, but better.

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:54.680
It has new operations,
and whatever.

00:09:54.680 --> 00:09:57.110
And then on the other
side is the runtime.

00:09:57.110 --> 00:10:00.460
Is that the separation
in-between Dataflow and Apache

00:10:00.460 --> 00:10:00.990
Beam?

00:10:00.990 --> 00:10:02.130
What are the differences there?

00:10:02.130 --> 00:10:04.630
FRANCES PERRY: So originally,
when we started with Dataflow,

00:10:04.630 --> 00:10:07.860
when I went in to GA in
2014, they were the same.

00:10:07.860 --> 00:10:10.680
We had the Data flow service
and the Dataflow SDK,

00:10:10.680 --> 00:10:12.750
which you used to program
against the service.

00:10:12.750 --> 00:10:15.519
But more recently, so
about last January--

00:10:15.519 --> 00:10:16.310
what would that be?

00:10:16.310 --> 00:10:17.310
2016?

00:10:17.310 --> 00:10:20.940
We donated those SDK to the
Apache Software Foundation

00:10:20.940 --> 00:10:23.880
and helped kickstart
the Apache Beam project.

00:10:23.880 --> 00:10:25.240
So now that is exactly true.

00:10:25.240 --> 00:10:27.390
Basically, Apache
Beam is this way

00:10:27.390 --> 00:10:29.430
of reasoning about
your programs.

00:10:29.430 --> 00:10:32.340
And Apache Beam pipelines
can run in multiple places.

00:10:32.340 --> 00:10:35.220
One of those places is on the
fully managed Cloud Dataflow

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:35.910
service.

00:10:35.910 --> 00:10:38.700
The other options are things
like on Apache Spark, Apache

00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:41.760
Flink, and other common
open-source run times.

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:43.440
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Let's go back to that.

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:45.340
We said that the
runtime is different.

00:10:45.340 --> 00:10:47.010
Like, if you're
running MapReduce,

00:10:47.010 --> 00:10:49.920
it's batching, while if
you're running Dataflow,

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:51.730
you get streaming.

00:10:51.730 --> 00:10:53.730
But what about the
programming model?

00:10:53.730 --> 00:10:55.950
What is the main
difference between those?

00:10:55.950 --> 00:10:58.830
Is there also like
map-shuffle-reduce phases?

00:10:58.830 --> 00:10:59.830
Or how does that work?

00:10:59.830 --> 00:11:01.020
FRANCES PERRY: So I
think there's really

00:11:01.020 --> 00:11:03.720
two big differences there
between sort of a MapReduce API

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:05.050
and what Beam gives you.

00:11:05.050 --> 00:11:08.130
The first one is that the way
you specify your business logic

00:11:08.130 --> 00:11:09.344
is higher level.

00:11:09.344 --> 00:11:11.010
When MapReduce came
out, people realized

00:11:11.010 --> 00:11:13.450
they could solve basically
any class of problem

00:11:13.450 --> 00:11:14.160
in a MapReduce.

00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:17.190
If you sort of took your
algorithm, twisted it enough,

00:11:17.190 --> 00:11:19.170
distorted it enough,
and shoved it

00:11:19.170 --> 00:11:21.760
into that map-shuffle-reduce
shuffle, reduce structure.

00:11:21.760 --> 00:11:24.910
So a lot of the newer systems,
including Spark and Flink

00:11:24.910 --> 00:11:26.940
and Cloud Dataflow and
Beam, are doing things

00:11:26.940 --> 00:11:29.190
where they give you a higher
level graph abstraction

00:11:29.190 --> 00:11:31.650
for talking about the
different transformations you

00:11:31.650 --> 00:11:33.180
want to do to your data.

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:35.220
And then we ensure
that the system is able

00:11:35.220 --> 00:11:37.920
to sort of optimize that down
into a lower-level thing.

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:41.100
So if you think about something
like function composition,

00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:43.890
if you want to take a data set,
and you want to go through it,

00:11:43.890 --> 00:11:45.660
and you want to
tokenize it to pieces.

00:11:45.660 --> 00:11:47.257
And then you want
to take each word,

00:11:47.257 --> 00:11:48.840
and you want to
lowercase them, right?

00:11:48.840 --> 00:11:50.923
When you writing that in
a MapReduce-style system,

00:11:50.923 --> 00:11:53.430
you would do that in a single
map operation, where you take

00:11:53.430 --> 00:11:55.620
the line of text, you
tokenize it, you lowercase it,

00:11:55.620 --> 00:11:57.276
and output those
lowercased words.

00:11:57.276 --> 00:11:58.650
What you're doing
there is you're

00:11:58.650 --> 00:12:02.970
being forced to hand-optimize,
or fuse, two unrelated pieces

00:12:02.970 --> 00:12:07.067
of logic into one method in
order to get the efficiency out

00:12:07.067 --> 00:12:07.650
of the system.

00:12:07.650 --> 00:12:09.608
Because you don't want
to go through large data

00:12:09.608 --> 00:12:11.190
sets multiple times.

00:12:11.190 --> 00:12:13.590
On the other hand, with these
newer styles of systems,

00:12:13.590 --> 00:12:16.740
we separate that out and let you
as the user write your business

00:12:16.740 --> 00:12:19.035
logic at a higher
level of abstraction.

00:12:19.035 --> 00:12:21.930
So you can think about
the logical phases

00:12:21.930 --> 00:12:24.360
not as they are most
efficient to execute,

00:12:24.360 --> 00:12:27.000
but as they make the most
sense for your algorithm

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:28.920
for expressing what
you're trying to do.

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:31.140
And then you let the system
do those optimizations

00:12:31.140 --> 00:12:32.552
to run them efficiently, right?

00:12:32.552 --> 00:12:34.260
And this is basically
the same difference

00:12:34.260 --> 00:12:36.090
that people have had
with assembly language

00:12:36.090 --> 00:12:38.440
and then higher-level
languages coming along later.

00:12:38.440 --> 00:12:41.430
You let the system do the boring
nastiness of really getting

00:12:41.430 --> 00:12:44.160
every ounce of efficiency
out, and you give somebody

00:12:44.160 --> 00:12:46.680
the right set of abstractions
to talk about what

00:12:46.680 --> 00:12:47.555
they're trying to do.

00:12:47.555 --> 00:12:49.013
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So, does that mean

00:12:49.013 --> 00:12:50.520
that what you're
doing then-- you're

00:12:50.520 --> 00:12:53.730
describing this graph,
like this pipeline,

00:12:53.730 --> 00:12:55.710
using the Apache Beam language.

00:12:55.710 --> 00:12:58.290
Well, let's call it a language,
like API, or whatever.

00:12:58.290 --> 00:12:59.040
FRANCES PERRY: Yep

00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:00.748
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And
that is Apache Beam.

00:13:00.748 --> 00:13:02.430
And then you
generate that graph.

00:13:02.430 --> 00:13:05.280
And how you run it is
completely independent.

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:08.910
And that's where you could run
it on Cloud Dataflow, or Spark,

00:13:08.910 --> 00:13:09.864
or any other runner.

00:13:09.864 --> 00:13:10.530
Is that correct?

00:13:10.530 --> 00:13:11.640
FRANCES PERRY: That's
exactly correct.

00:13:11.640 --> 00:13:11.850
Yep.

00:13:11.850 --> 00:13:12.880
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That is very cool.

00:13:12.880 --> 00:13:14.880
MARK MANDEL: So, I'm actually
curious about one actual thing

00:13:14.880 --> 00:13:16.630
as well, too, about
finding the right spot

00:13:16.630 --> 00:13:18.620
for using these tools.

00:13:18.620 --> 00:13:20.520
Quite often, when
we talk about people

00:13:20.520 --> 00:13:22.110
who have gone to
higher abstractions

00:13:22.110 --> 00:13:24.270
or other technologies
other than MapReduce

00:13:24.270 --> 00:13:26.670
for doing these sort of big
data, data-set-type stuff.

00:13:26.670 --> 00:13:29.120
We talk about BigQuery a
lot for obvious reasons.

00:13:29.120 --> 00:13:31.896
So, as a general
rule of thumb, where

00:13:31.896 --> 00:13:33.270
do you find that
people are like,

00:13:33.270 --> 00:13:36.344
oh, Dataflow is a better fit
for this, whereas BigQuery's

00:13:36.344 --> 00:13:37.260
a better fit for this.

00:13:37.260 --> 00:13:38.620
Where do you draw that line?

00:13:38.620 --> 00:13:41.130
FRANCES PERRY: Well, I think
they do absolutely overlap

00:13:41.130 --> 00:13:44.190
in some use cases, but
I think if you've ever

00:13:44.190 --> 00:13:46.740
tried to write some really
complex SQL queries,

00:13:46.740 --> 00:13:48.690
you will hit up against
the bounds of what

00:13:48.690 --> 00:13:51.370
that abstraction is
really suitable for,

00:13:51.370 --> 00:13:53.100
at least for mere mortals.

00:13:53.100 --> 00:13:56.340
So something like
Beam does give you

00:13:56.340 --> 00:13:58.164
a little bit more
flexibility there.

00:13:58.164 --> 00:14:00.330
But I think there is a lot
of interest, for example,

00:14:00.330 --> 00:14:02.040
in the Beam community,
in writing a SQL

00:14:02.040 --> 00:14:04.030
DSL that runs on top of Beam.

00:14:04.030 --> 00:14:05.599
So they are quite related.

00:14:05.599 --> 00:14:07.890
There's been additional
functionality added to BigQuery

00:14:07.890 --> 00:14:09.042
to get better join support.

00:14:09.042 --> 00:14:11.250
And there's been a lot of
improvements over the years

00:14:11.250 --> 00:14:13.650
that have brought these two
systems closer together.

00:14:13.650 --> 00:14:15.650
But really, I think
that there's less

00:14:15.650 --> 00:14:17.400
of a cliff when you're
running a Dataflow,

00:14:17.400 --> 00:14:20.220
like the types of things you
can do are just more flexible.

00:14:20.220 --> 00:14:23.366
You can integrate with much
more different data sources,

00:14:23.366 --> 00:14:25.740
the streaming, the real-time
capabilities that are there.

00:14:25.740 --> 00:14:28.160
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So, since
you're mentioning data sources,

00:14:28.160 --> 00:14:30.450
I think that that's
a interesting thing.

00:14:30.450 --> 00:14:32.130
Like, we talked about pipelines.

00:14:32.130 --> 00:14:33.720
What do we connect, normally?

00:14:33.720 --> 00:14:34.320
Is it--

00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:35.695
FRANCES PERRY:
Anything you want.

00:14:35.695 --> 00:14:36.390
[CHUCKLING]

00:14:36.390 --> 00:14:37.100
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Anything we want?

00:14:37.100 --> 00:14:37.840
MARK MANDEL: Anything.

00:14:37.840 --> 00:14:39.590
I'm connecting my
[INAUDIBLE] to Dataflow.

00:14:39.590 --> 00:14:40.230
[CHUCKLING]

00:14:40.230 --> 00:14:42.140
FRANCES PERRY: Go for it.

00:14:42.140 --> 00:14:45.600
Beam is really designed
to be extensible

00:14:45.600 --> 00:14:47.110
in many different ways.

00:14:47.110 --> 00:14:49.742
And so, one of those
is the input format

00:14:49.742 --> 00:14:50.700
that you're writing to.

00:14:50.700 --> 00:14:53.170
So, Beam has an API
called the source API

00:14:53.170 --> 00:14:56.341
that you can use to teach
Beam about a new data format.

00:14:56.341 --> 00:14:58.590
And the reason you need to
sort of teach it explicitly

00:14:58.590 --> 00:15:02.730
about the format is that we
want to make it very easy to run

00:15:02.730 --> 00:15:04.820
this efficiently in parallel.

00:15:04.820 --> 00:15:07.210
So if you think about
a new data format,

00:15:07.210 --> 00:15:10.060
you need to be able to
answer questions like, meh,

00:15:10.060 --> 00:15:12.070
how big is that data format?

00:15:12.070 --> 00:15:13.759
How would you split
it into x pieces?

00:15:13.759 --> 00:15:15.550
And then how would you
read a piece, right?

00:15:15.550 --> 00:15:17.133
And those are really
what are required

00:15:17.133 --> 00:15:19.180
for the different
runners behind Beam

00:15:19.180 --> 00:15:21.850
to take that and parallelize an
event across multiple workers--

00:15:21.850 --> 00:15:24.430
sort of chunk that data
set up into pieces,

00:15:24.430 --> 00:15:26.530
and hand them out to
different workers.

00:15:26.530 --> 00:15:29.860
One thing Beam does that's more
advanced than many of the older

00:15:29.860 --> 00:15:33.520
APIs out there is adds a notion
of what we call dynamic work

00:15:33.520 --> 00:15:34.790
rebalancing.

00:15:34.790 --> 00:15:38.710
So, as you're teaching Beam
about a new input format

00:15:38.710 --> 00:15:42.060
and you're telling it how you're
reading a chunk of that format,

00:15:42.060 --> 00:15:45.550
Beam also asks you to provide
signals about your progress

00:15:45.550 --> 00:15:48.220
through that, and then
potentially stop early.

00:15:48.220 --> 00:15:51.340
So what that lets the system do
is divide the input set into 10

00:15:51.340 --> 00:15:53.830
pieces, farm them
out to 10 workers,

00:15:53.830 --> 00:15:56.160
realize that one of the
workers is really, really slow,

00:15:56.160 --> 00:15:59.080
and tell that worker, actually
you're only 20% of your way

00:15:59.080 --> 00:15:59.950
through your data.

00:15:59.950 --> 00:16:01.480
Can you please stop at 40%?

00:16:01.480 --> 00:16:04.450
I'm going to steal that last
60% and reassign it to somebody

00:16:04.450 --> 00:16:06.040
else who's got idle cycles.

00:16:06.040 --> 00:16:08.650
Dynamic work rebalancing
has been a huge thing

00:16:08.650 --> 00:16:10.450
for improving
straggler shards, which

00:16:10.450 --> 00:16:13.870
is this really common problem
in distributed data processing,

00:16:13.870 --> 00:16:16.330
where either because of
slow machines or data

00:16:16.330 --> 00:16:18.010
characteristics
or something, you

00:16:18.010 --> 00:16:20.620
end up with like a huge number
of parallel machines sitting

00:16:20.620 --> 00:16:22.580
idle while one
machine chunks away

00:16:22.580 --> 00:16:23.800
on the remainder of the work.

00:16:23.800 --> 00:16:25.490
MARK MANDEL: So,
going to assume there

00:16:25.490 --> 00:16:28.066
are a bunch of like
connectors for like GCP stuff,

00:16:28.066 --> 00:16:29.190
like I'm guessing Pub/Sub--

00:16:29.190 --> 00:16:29.440
FRANCES PERRY: Oh, yeah.

00:16:29.440 --> 00:16:30.280
MARK MANDEL: Things like that.

00:16:30.280 --> 00:16:32.380
Does that mean then you
have to like teach it

00:16:32.380 --> 00:16:33.430
how to use Pub/Sub?

00:16:33.430 --> 00:16:35.050
Or does that work
out of the box?

00:16:35.050 --> 00:16:36.190
FRANCES PERRY: Yeah, thank
you for clarifying that.

00:16:36.190 --> 00:16:38.180
We have a lot of
built-in connectors.

00:16:38.180 --> 00:16:39.760
Your standard GCP
ones are there--

00:16:39.760 --> 00:16:43.300
BigQuery, Pub/Sub, all the
various textual formats which

00:16:43.300 --> 00:16:44.655
can, of course, come from GCS.

00:16:44.655 --> 00:16:46.030
One of the things
that I'm really

00:16:46.030 --> 00:16:49.220
excited about kickstarting
the Apache Beam ecosystem

00:16:49.220 --> 00:16:51.370
is really that
open-source environment

00:16:51.370 --> 00:16:53.380
is where the diversity
of connectors

00:16:53.380 --> 00:16:54.550
is really coming from.

00:16:54.550 --> 00:16:56.890
So since we sort of kickstarted
Beam in the last year,

00:16:56.890 --> 00:17:00.250
we probably added
15 new connectors.

00:17:00.250 --> 00:17:02.860
There's Cassandra
and Kafka and Kinesis

00:17:02.860 --> 00:17:04.420
and all sorts of
things coming out

00:17:04.420 --> 00:17:07.630
of that, which I think is
really great for helping people

00:17:07.630 --> 00:17:10.329
get their data into
the systems that they

00:17:10.329 --> 00:17:11.530
need to process it in.

00:17:11.530 --> 00:17:13.988
MARK MANDEL: Sounds like there's
a great relationship there

00:17:13.988 --> 00:17:15.950
between Apache
Beam and Dataflow.

00:17:15.950 --> 00:17:17.500
So on the Dataflow
side, I'll ask,

00:17:17.500 --> 00:17:20.270
like what integrations
with other GCP systems--

00:17:20.270 --> 00:17:22.770
you just talked about that sort
of stuff, but is there like,

00:17:22.770 --> 00:17:24.339
I don't know, logging,
or alerting, or--

00:17:24.339 --> 00:17:25.329
FRANCES PERRY: Absolutely.

00:17:25.329 --> 00:17:26.109
MARK MANDEL: What
integrations does

00:17:26.109 --> 00:17:27.640
it have with GCP specifically
on Dataflow's side?

00:17:27.640 --> 00:17:29.098
FRANCES PERRY: Yep,
so we're trying

00:17:29.098 --> 00:17:30.550
to integrate as
tightly as we can

00:17:30.550 --> 00:17:32.270
with so many of the
other systems, right?

00:17:32.270 --> 00:17:34.990
Because when you come
to GCP as a platform,

00:17:34.990 --> 00:17:37.780
what you're really looking
for is for all those systems

00:17:37.780 --> 00:17:39.310
to work beautifully together.

00:17:39.310 --> 00:17:41.830
What happens when you
actually launch a Dataflow job

00:17:41.830 --> 00:17:44.750
is that you write an
Apache Beam pipeline,

00:17:44.750 --> 00:17:46.540
and you're running a
program somewhere--

00:17:46.540 --> 00:17:49.150
could be locally, on a server
of your own somewhere-- that's

00:17:49.150 --> 00:17:51.480
describing the shape
of this data processing

00:17:51.480 --> 00:17:54.081
DAG, this graph that
you want to run.

00:17:54.081 --> 00:17:56.080
So then you-- what happens
with that-- that gets

00:17:56.080 --> 00:18:00.120
serialized into JSON and
sent to the Dataflow service.

00:18:00.120 --> 00:18:02.440
And this is the description,
sort of the outline

00:18:02.440 --> 00:18:04.060
of the processing.

00:18:04.060 --> 00:18:05.980
And some of that
processing will include

00:18:05.980 --> 00:18:08.080
serialized bits of user code.

00:18:08.080 --> 00:18:10.670
This is the actual things you
want to do for each element,

00:18:10.670 --> 00:18:11.169
right?

00:18:11.169 --> 00:18:13.670
But from the Dataflow
system's perspective,

00:18:13.670 --> 00:18:15.546
it basically has a plan
or a map-- you know,

00:18:15.546 --> 00:18:18.040
a plan of the
different processing.

00:18:18.040 --> 00:18:21.330
It then spins up a
bunch of GCE workers

00:18:21.330 --> 00:18:23.740
who it will actually farm
all that work out to.

00:18:23.740 --> 00:18:27.010
So your code that you've written
to process all your elements

00:18:27.010 --> 00:18:29.709
is happening on GCE
VMs, but those VMs

00:18:29.709 --> 00:18:32.250
are going to be fully managed
and auto-scaled by the Dataflow

00:18:32.250 --> 00:18:33.280
service.

00:18:33.280 --> 00:18:35.900
So you don't need to worry too
much about how to manage them,

00:18:35.900 --> 00:18:37.358
but they're there
if you need them.

00:18:37.358 --> 00:18:39.920
As those VMs are processing
each of your elements,

00:18:39.920 --> 00:18:41.534
they're logging
into Cloud Logging.

00:18:41.534 --> 00:18:43.450
And then we have some
really great integration

00:18:43.450 --> 00:18:46.470
with Cloud Logging that
lets you very easily search

00:18:46.470 --> 00:18:48.280
your logs right
in the Dataflow UI

00:18:48.280 --> 00:18:52.120
and see what different portions
of your pipeline are logging.

00:18:52.120 --> 00:18:54.280
We're adding some
additional integration

00:18:54.280 --> 00:18:57.220
with different
tools in Stackdriver

00:18:57.220 --> 00:18:59.650
to get better monitoring
of how your watermarks are

00:18:59.650 --> 00:19:02.740
going, what your errors are,
all these types of things.

00:19:02.740 --> 00:19:04.900
FRANCESC CAMPOY: We've
covered the input.

00:19:04.900 --> 00:19:07.000
We've covered the--
while it's running,

00:19:07.000 --> 00:19:08.500
how you can see what's going on.

00:19:08.500 --> 00:19:09.090
FRANCES PERRY: Yup.

00:19:09.090 --> 00:19:10.714
FRANCESC CAMPOY: What
about the output?

00:19:10.714 --> 00:19:13.060
Do you store it in a file?

00:19:13.060 --> 00:19:13.986
Can you choose?

00:19:13.986 --> 00:19:15.610
Or is it more like
you were mentioning,

00:19:15.610 --> 00:19:18.170
that there was like SQL
statements that you can run?

00:19:18.170 --> 00:19:19.120
FRANCES PERRY: So,
it basically, just

00:19:19.120 --> 00:19:21.410
like you can teach a system
about new kinds of input

00:19:21.410 --> 00:19:23.890
formats and we have a bunch
of common ones built in,

00:19:23.890 --> 00:19:25.840
same thing will
hold for outputs.

00:19:25.840 --> 00:19:29.530
So, you know, you can very
easily write to BigQuery GCS,

00:19:29.530 --> 00:19:30.390
Pub/Sub--

00:19:30.390 --> 00:19:31.900
you know, your standard things.

00:19:31.900 --> 00:19:33.689
And also, if you
have something else

00:19:33.689 --> 00:19:35.230
you need to do--
you want to call out

00:19:35.230 --> 00:19:38.259
to a third-party system or
write a custom file format

00:19:38.259 --> 00:19:40.300
or something like that--
you have the flexibility

00:19:40.300 --> 00:19:42.091
to teach the system
about additional things

00:19:42.091 --> 00:19:42.760
there as well.

00:19:42.760 --> 00:19:43.210
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Interesting.

00:19:43.210 --> 00:19:44.840
So, does that mean
that, for instance,

00:19:44.840 --> 00:19:48.340
if I'm doing streaming, how
often do I write to the output?

00:19:48.340 --> 00:19:51.190
FRANCES PERRY: That's going to
be very application-specific.

00:19:51.190 --> 00:19:54.490
What we liked about Beam is
that you write your application

00:19:54.490 --> 00:19:57.470
logic, sort of the thing you're
doing to your program, once.

00:19:57.470 --> 00:19:59.140
And then it's very
easy to customize

00:19:59.140 --> 00:20:03.580
that same algorithm for
different latency situations.

00:20:03.580 --> 00:20:04.940
So if you think about writing--

00:20:04.940 --> 00:20:06.880
go back to our mobile
gaming example.

00:20:06.880 --> 00:20:09.150
If you think about
writing a computation that

00:20:09.150 --> 00:20:13.350
sums the scores per team,
you write that transformation

00:20:13.350 --> 00:20:17.040
just as if it were taking a
big collection, a PCollection,

00:20:17.040 --> 00:20:20.940
of team and score events,
and basically summing them up

00:20:20.940 --> 00:20:23.070
across unique teams.

00:20:23.070 --> 00:20:25.770
Then when you run that, you
can apply it in different ways.

00:20:25.770 --> 00:20:27.270
You can window it,
which means you

00:20:27.270 --> 00:20:30.220
want that computation applied
over different slices of event

00:20:30.220 --> 00:20:30.720
times.

00:20:30.720 --> 00:20:32.850
You can say, take that
into your summation

00:20:32.850 --> 00:20:37.130
and give me a sum per hour,
or per minute, or per day,

00:20:37.130 --> 00:20:39.390
and choose the scope of that.

00:20:39.390 --> 00:20:41.410
You can also customize
the triggering,

00:20:41.410 --> 00:20:45.089
which is how the relationship
to processing time

00:20:45.089 --> 00:20:45.755
comes into play.

00:20:45.755 --> 00:20:48.900
So you can say, I want you
to sum it over all time,

00:20:48.900 --> 00:20:51.540
but I want to know an update
of what the current score is

00:20:51.540 --> 00:20:52.720
every hour.

00:20:52.720 --> 00:20:55.350
So I want you to sort of
accumulate over all time,

00:20:55.350 --> 00:20:59.040
over the bigger window,
but I want smaller updates.

00:20:59.040 --> 00:21:02.222
You can also say, and I want
to know if late data comes in.

00:21:02.222 --> 00:21:04.680
Because one of the things you'll
find in a streaming system

00:21:04.680 --> 00:21:07.940
is somebody's playing our mobile
game on a transatlantic flight.

00:21:07.940 --> 00:21:09.330
They're in airplane mode.

00:21:09.330 --> 00:21:10.350
And then they land.

00:21:10.350 --> 00:21:13.500
Four or five hours later, they
have to reconnect to a network.

00:21:13.500 --> 00:21:16.830
And so you're getting a score
that comes into the system

00:21:16.830 --> 00:21:17.806
multiple hours delayed.

00:21:17.806 --> 00:21:19.680
And you have to figure
out how to adjust that

00:21:19.680 --> 00:21:22.470
into the computations that
you've already been performing.

00:21:22.470 --> 00:21:23.386
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:21:23.386 --> 00:21:26.400
So, you said something
that took me back

00:21:26.400 --> 00:21:28.857
to the time I wrote a
Cloud Dataflow job--

00:21:28.857 --> 00:21:29.940
FRANCES PERRY: [CHUCKLING]

00:21:29.940 --> 00:21:31.920
FRANCESC CAMPOY: --which
is that PCollection.

00:21:31.920 --> 00:21:33.090
FRANCES PERRY: Yeah.

00:21:33.090 --> 00:21:35.260
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And I'm
actually a Go developer,

00:21:35.260 --> 00:21:39.210
and I know that PCollection is
a very, very Java thing to say.

00:21:39.210 --> 00:21:41.070
I know that you
can run Dataflow.

00:21:41.070 --> 00:21:44.130
Well, you can program
[INAUDIBLE] Beam pipelines

00:21:44.130 --> 00:21:45.135
with Java.

00:21:45.135 --> 00:21:45.420
FRANCES PERRY: Yeah.

00:21:45.420 --> 00:21:46.750
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Is there
any other languages,

00:21:46.750 --> 00:21:47.880
and what is the plan in there?

00:21:47.880 --> 00:21:49.020
FRANCES PERRY: Right,
so I think that one

00:21:49.020 --> 00:21:51.660
of the things we're really
excited about with Beam

00:21:51.660 --> 00:21:54.600
is that we've designed the
system from the beginning

00:21:54.600 --> 00:21:57.510
to really focus on
extensibility and portability

00:21:57.510 --> 00:22:00.000
to the point where it's
designed to support

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:03.810
multiple SDKs as the community
is interested in writing them.

00:22:03.810 --> 00:22:07.380
Those SDKs really are just
generating that graph, right,

00:22:07.380 --> 00:22:09.810
which is itself just a
language-agnostic plan

00:22:09.810 --> 00:22:12.450
or representation
of the computation.

00:22:12.450 --> 00:22:15.090
It's only the little bits of
user code in that plan that

00:22:15.090 --> 00:22:16.450
are a specific language.

00:22:16.450 --> 00:22:19.200
So it's actually quite
an extensible format.

00:22:19.200 --> 00:22:22.830
And then the different
runners can take that plan

00:22:22.830 --> 00:22:24.794
and go ahead and
run it very easily.

00:22:24.794 --> 00:22:27.210
And then they just need to
know how to basically call back

00:22:27.210 --> 00:22:31.090
into the specific language
on a per-element basis.

00:22:31.090 --> 00:22:33.420
So what that enables
is the community

00:22:33.420 --> 00:22:37.710
to really grow by adding new
SDKs, and setting them up so

00:22:37.710 --> 00:22:41.250
that new SDKs can run across
all the different runners.

00:22:41.250 --> 00:22:43.040
So, that's the
vision-- that it's

00:22:43.040 --> 00:22:45.510
a fully mix and match of
choose an SDK, choose a runner,

00:22:45.510 --> 00:22:46.530
life is good.

00:22:46.530 --> 00:22:48.930
Visions are never--
they're always a journey.

00:22:48.930 --> 00:22:50.880
[LAUGHS] So where
Beam is at right

00:22:50.880 --> 00:22:55.260
now is that we have a Java SDK
that ports across, I think,

00:22:55.260 --> 00:22:58.530
four different runners
in the main line branch,

00:22:58.530 --> 00:23:01.660
and another runner on
a development branch.

00:23:01.660 --> 00:23:03.700
And then we also
have a Python SDK

00:23:03.700 --> 00:23:06.110
that's fully merged
on the master branch,

00:23:06.110 --> 00:23:08.376
but we don't yet have the
portability story totally

00:23:08.376 --> 00:23:09.250
fleshed out for that.

00:23:09.250 --> 00:23:11.409
So the Python SDK right
now can run locally

00:23:11.409 --> 00:23:13.200
for development and
testing, and it can run

00:23:13.200 --> 00:23:14.910
on the Cloud Dataflow service.

00:23:14.910 --> 00:23:16.410
And the community's
actively working

00:23:16.410 --> 00:23:18.618
towards getting that running
across the other runners

00:23:18.618 --> 00:23:19.210
as well.

00:23:19.210 --> 00:23:22.920
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So, what does
a pipeline look like when you've

00:23:22.920 --> 00:23:24.690
described it in a language?

00:23:24.690 --> 00:23:28.710
Meaning, is there like some
JSON way of communicating that

00:23:28.710 --> 00:23:30.630
to the runtime?

00:23:30.630 --> 00:23:32.317
Is it like XML, or what?

00:23:32.317 --> 00:23:34.650
FRANCES PERRY: Beam will have
a language-agnostic format

00:23:34.650 --> 00:23:36.420
that it can use to
do that interchange.

00:23:36.420 --> 00:23:37.830
For the users, we
won't have them

00:23:37.830 --> 00:23:39.150
interact with that directly.

00:23:39.150 --> 00:23:42.130
They're taking a
conceptual abstraction,

00:23:42.130 --> 00:23:44.010
which is what Beam
model really is, right?

00:23:44.010 --> 00:23:47.665
This idea of PCollections, and
parallel dos, group by keys,

00:23:47.665 --> 00:23:49.290
windowing, triggering--
we haven't even

00:23:49.290 --> 00:23:50.540
gone through all of these yet.

00:23:50.540 --> 00:23:53.160
And materializing it
in different languages

00:23:53.160 --> 00:23:55.980
for specific user communities
is a challenging problem,

00:23:55.980 --> 00:23:59.580
because you want to make sure
that the abstractions feel

00:23:59.580 --> 00:24:00.300
native, right?

00:24:00.300 --> 00:24:03.120
When in Python, do as
the Pythonians do--

00:24:03.120 --> 00:24:04.110
Something like that.

00:24:04.110 --> 00:24:08.340
You don't want it to feel like
it's a Java abstraction forced

00:24:08.340 --> 00:24:10.020
into a different format.

00:24:10.020 --> 00:24:11.940
So, that takes sort
of a subtle touch

00:24:11.940 --> 00:24:14.920
to both make the abstractions
clear as to what they are,

00:24:14.920 --> 00:24:18.485
and also feel native to the
different language communities.

00:24:18.485 --> 00:24:19.610
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very nice.

00:24:19.610 --> 00:24:20.485
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:24:20.485 --> 00:24:21.780
So, we talked a bit--

00:24:21.780 --> 00:24:23.100
we've talked about some
of the technical stuff.

00:24:23.100 --> 00:24:24.550
And like, we've
talked about Dataflow.

00:24:24.550 --> 00:24:25.500
We've talked about Beam.

00:24:25.500 --> 00:24:26.000
I'm curious.

00:24:26.000 --> 00:24:28.950
If Beam is running inside
the Apache Foundation,

00:24:28.950 --> 00:24:31.080
how is the organization
structured there?

00:24:31.080 --> 00:24:33.630
I'm guessing we're still
heavily involved there, like--

00:24:33.630 --> 00:24:33.840
FRANCES PERRY: Absolutely.

00:24:33.840 --> 00:24:35.070
MARK MANDEL: What's that
open-source community look

00:24:35.070 --> 00:24:35.630
like?

00:24:35.630 --> 00:24:37.470
FRANCES PERRY: So,
Google have the bulk

00:24:37.470 --> 00:24:40.530
of the code for the
original Dataflow Java SDK.

00:24:40.530 --> 00:24:43.290
And we had a couple of other
partners we were working with--

00:24:43.290 --> 00:24:45.450
Cloudera and data
Artisans, and Talon--

00:24:45.450 --> 00:24:47.430
who are writing
runners and other sort

00:24:47.430 --> 00:24:50.040
of things that could
integrate with this SDK.

00:24:50.040 --> 00:24:53.100
And so together, the group of
us basically proposed the Apache

00:24:53.100 --> 00:24:55.320
Beam project to the
Apache Software Foundation

00:24:55.320 --> 00:24:57.744
and donated this sort
of initial codebase.

00:24:57.744 --> 00:24:59.160
And since then,
we've really grown

00:24:59.160 --> 00:25:00.487
into a full-fledged community.

00:25:00.487 --> 00:25:02.820
When you're working with the
Apache Software Foundation,

00:25:02.820 --> 00:25:05.850
it's very reach an
open community driven

00:25:05.850 --> 00:25:06.850
by individuals.

00:25:06.850 --> 00:25:09.150
So it's not specific
corporations doing things,

00:25:09.150 --> 00:25:12.250
but you're really acting
on behalf of the project.

00:25:12.250 --> 00:25:14.490
So I think one of the
things we're proudest of

00:25:14.490 --> 00:25:17.490
is how we were able to take
something that was originally

00:25:17.490 --> 00:25:20.100
basically a proprietary,
single-company thing,

00:25:20.100 --> 00:25:23.730
and start to transition
it into an open ecosystem,

00:25:23.730 --> 00:25:26.520
with a whole bunch of diverse
perspectives, new people coming

00:25:26.520 --> 00:25:28.920
in, being welcomed
into the community.

00:25:28.920 --> 00:25:30.890
That's been quite the
journey for the last year

00:25:30.890 --> 00:25:32.360
for many of us.

00:25:32.360 --> 00:25:34.860
MARK MANDEL: OK, and I'll ask
you because I'm quite curious.

00:25:34.860 --> 00:25:38.100
What's the most interesting
or crazy or imaginative

00:25:38.100 --> 00:25:40.560
thing that you've seen
somebody do with Dataflow?

00:25:40.560 --> 00:25:41.160
FRANCES PERRY:
You know, I always

00:25:41.160 --> 00:25:42.660
have trouble answering
this question

00:25:42.660 --> 00:25:45.060
because it's such a
general framework that you

00:25:45.060 --> 00:25:47.130
can do basically anything.

00:25:47.130 --> 00:25:50.970
I think, for me, the thing
that is the most compelling

00:25:50.970 --> 00:25:54.510
is really the mobile
gaming application,

00:25:54.510 --> 00:25:58.260
because what I really like about
that is it brings home pretty

00:25:58.260 --> 00:26:00.360
easily, I think, the
complexities of working

00:26:00.360 --> 00:26:03.000
in these real-time
distributed systems.

00:26:03.000 --> 00:26:05.580
If you go and look on
the Cloud Dataflow site,

00:26:05.580 --> 00:26:07.590
we have a mobile
gaming walkthrough,

00:26:07.590 --> 00:26:09.240
which what we do
there is show you

00:26:09.240 --> 00:26:12.130
how you build a simple batch
pipeline to do your summations

00:26:12.130 --> 00:26:14.310
of team scores or something.

00:26:14.310 --> 00:26:16.380
And then you start
adjusting until by the end,

00:26:16.380 --> 00:26:19.980
you've got a really complex
streaming pipeline that's

00:26:19.980 --> 00:26:23.910
working over real-time data,
ruling out spam robots.

00:26:23.910 --> 00:26:25.800
What we've built is
something that can really

00:26:25.800 --> 00:26:27.990
give you a gentle ramp
to learning and adding

00:26:27.990 --> 00:26:29.280
these new technologies.

00:26:29.280 --> 00:26:31.080
And that gives you
so much flexibility

00:26:31.080 --> 00:26:34.800
for how you, as a developer,
adjust your programs

00:26:34.800 --> 00:26:36.540
as things move along--

00:26:36.540 --> 00:26:37.770
as your needs change.

00:26:37.770 --> 00:26:39.020
Because they will, right?

00:26:39.020 --> 00:26:41.103
People have been doing
batch for a very long time.

00:26:41.103 --> 00:26:42.690
People know that
they should probably

00:26:42.690 --> 00:26:45.480
be doing stream processing,
but it's kind of hard.

00:26:45.480 --> 00:26:46.812
It's kind of complex.

00:26:46.812 --> 00:26:48.270
So what we really
want to do is let

00:26:48.270 --> 00:26:50.610
you sort of pick and
choose, and as your needs

00:26:50.610 --> 00:26:55.290
change, be able to very flexibly
shift your style of processing.

00:26:55.290 --> 00:26:56.040
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:26:56.040 --> 00:26:57.831
So, we're running out
of time a little bit,

00:26:57.831 --> 00:26:59.880
but has there been
anything that we haven't

00:26:59.880 --> 00:27:01.950
had a chance to mention, or
you want to make sure you plug,

00:27:01.950 --> 00:27:03.540
or make sure the
listeners hear about?

00:27:03.540 --> 00:27:04.873
FRANCES PERRY: Yeah, I think I--

00:27:04.873 --> 00:27:06.420
since I'm on the
SDK side of things,

00:27:06.420 --> 00:27:08.610
I haven't talked too much
about the Dataflow service

00:27:08.610 --> 00:27:11.020
and it's no-knobs
approach to things.

00:27:11.020 --> 00:27:14.580
So I think one of the
things that people realize

00:27:14.580 --> 00:27:18.090
is maintaining and tuning your
own cluster can [INAUDIBLE] not

00:27:18.090 --> 00:27:20.460
only is it a pain to
manage those things,

00:27:20.460 --> 00:27:22.380
you also end up
needing to change

00:27:22.380 --> 00:27:26.790
that tuning as your data sizes
change or your processing

00:27:26.790 --> 00:27:27.840
changes, right?

00:27:27.840 --> 00:27:30.930
So one of the things that's
really exciting about Dataflow

00:27:30.930 --> 00:27:33.450
is that it's fully
autotuning, so it

00:27:33.450 --> 00:27:35.490
will choose the
number of workers

00:27:35.490 --> 00:27:38.070
that your application--
your pipeline needs to run,

00:27:38.070 --> 00:27:39.930
and it can change
that over time, right?

00:27:39.930 --> 00:27:43.230
And that's either because
you've got a streaming pipeline,

00:27:43.230 --> 00:27:46.112
and you had a huge spike
in user traffic, right?

00:27:46.112 --> 00:27:47.820
And all of a sudden,
you're going to need

00:27:47.820 --> 00:27:50.190
to drastically scale up, right?

00:27:50.190 --> 00:27:53.100
Or you're processing a
large number of logs,

00:27:53.100 --> 00:27:55.740
and you drastically filter it
down into just a small data set

00:27:55.740 --> 00:27:57.031
and then expand that out again.

00:27:57.031 --> 00:27:59.289
So even though your pipeline
might run for 12 hours,

00:27:59.289 --> 00:28:01.080
there might be a period
there in the middle

00:28:01.080 --> 00:28:03.082
where there's very
little processing to do.

00:28:03.082 --> 00:28:04.290
You want to be able to take--

00:28:04.290 --> 00:28:06.150
throw a lot of machines
out at the beginning,

00:28:06.150 --> 00:28:08.233
scale them down while
they're idle because there's

00:28:08.233 --> 00:28:10.740
no additional data to process,
and then scale them back up

00:28:10.740 --> 00:28:11.685
as you need to.

00:28:11.685 --> 00:28:11.842
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:28:11.842 --> 00:28:12.610
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:28:12.610 --> 00:28:14.776
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That sounds
like a great experience

00:28:14.776 --> 00:28:15.930
for developers.

00:28:15.930 --> 00:28:17.670
So, one last question.

00:28:17.670 --> 00:28:20.280
For people that want to learn
more about Dataflow, Apache

00:28:20.280 --> 00:28:22.039
Beam, what's the link?

00:28:22.039 --> 00:28:23.580
FRANCES PERRY: Yeah,
so I would start

00:28:23.580 --> 00:28:25.920
on cloud.google.com/dataflow.

00:28:25.920 --> 00:28:29.490
That's got all your basics about
the Cloud Dataflow project.

00:28:29.490 --> 00:28:31.639
For Beam, you'd go
to beam.apache.org.

00:28:31.639 --> 00:28:32.680
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Perfect.

00:28:32.680 --> 00:28:34.471
Well, thank you so much
for taking the time

00:28:34.471 --> 00:28:37.170
today to talk to us and
explain a little bit more

00:28:37.170 --> 00:28:40.240
about what Cloud Dataflow
and Apache Beam are.

00:28:40.240 --> 00:28:40.740
Thank you.

00:28:40.740 --> 00:28:41.070
FRANCES PERRY: Excellent.

00:28:41.070 --> 00:28:41.790
Thanks, guys.

00:28:41.790 --> 00:28:44.340
MARK MANDEL: So, thanks again
to Frances for joining us today

00:28:44.340 --> 00:28:46.140
to tell us all about Dataflow.

00:28:46.140 --> 00:28:48.455
Really great conversation,
and really interesting,

00:28:48.455 --> 00:28:50.830
especially because I don't
play as much with the big data

00:28:50.830 --> 00:28:53.580
space, so I really enjoy
hearing about what capabilities

00:28:53.580 --> 00:28:56.430
we have in this area for
big data and analytics,

00:28:56.430 --> 00:28:57.930
and especially streaming data.

