00:29:17.731 --> 00:29:21.060
Anyway, [LAUGHS] it's time
for our question of the week,

00:29:21.060 --> 00:29:24.810
comes from one of our
listeners, Chirag Nayyar.

00:29:24.810 --> 00:29:26.960
And he's asking about--

00:29:26.960 --> 00:29:29.700
OK, so I'm trying to set up
my web application with Cloud

00:29:29.700 --> 00:29:31.280
SQL, which is a great idea.

00:29:31.280 --> 00:29:32.910
If you need to run
a SQL database,

00:29:32.910 --> 00:29:35.490
Cloud SQL is a great
place to do so.

00:29:35.490 --> 00:29:38.370
He's saying that the app is
running on GCE, Google Compute

00:29:38.370 --> 00:29:41.100
Engine, and he wants
to implement autoscaler

00:29:41.100 --> 00:29:42.480
and managed instance groups.

00:29:42.480 --> 00:29:43.950
Now, the problem
is that when you

00:29:43.950 --> 00:29:46.860
want to set up a
connection with Cloud SQL,

00:29:46.860 --> 00:29:50.040
one of the ways of doing
this is to whitelist

00:29:50.040 --> 00:29:53.820
the IP of the instance
that will connect to it.

00:29:53.820 --> 00:29:56.430
Now, if you have a
managed instance group,

00:29:56.430 --> 00:29:58.496
you do not know necessarily
how many instances

00:29:58.496 --> 00:29:59.370
you're going to have.

00:29:59.370 --> 00:30:02.070
So, how do you do this?

00:30:02.070 --> 00:30:04.016
And I came up with a solution.

00:30:04.016 --> 00:30:05.890
And I'm going to say it
first, but then we're

00:30:05.890 --> 00:30:10.180
going to talk about the actual
good solution that you gave.

00:30:10.180 --> 00:30:12.580
And my solution was,
well, if there's

00:30:12.580 --> 00:30:15.730
any way you can know
what is the range of IPs

00:30:15.730 --> 00:30:17.560
that your instances
will have, then

00:30:17.560 --> 00:30:21.130
it should be quite easy
to just simply whitelist

00:30:21.130 --> 00:30:23.620
the range of IPs
that you might have.

00:30:23.620 --> 00:30:25.180
But now, the
problem is that what

00:30:25.180 --> 00:30:27.374
if that range is limitless?

00:30:27.374 --> 00:30:29.290
Like, you don't really
know how many instances

00:30:29.290 --> 00:30:30.165
you're going to have.

00:30:30.165 --> 00:30:32.650
You may have two and maybe
12, but at some point,

00:30:32.650 --> 00:30:34.330
maybe you'll have in the 20.

00:30:34.330 --> 00:30:37.155
And then those eight
that you didn't count on,

00:30:37.155 --> 00:30:38.780
well, they will not
be able to connect.

00:30:38.780 --> 00:30:41.200
So, But is not really a
very scalable solution.

00:30:41.200 --> 00:30:43.300
So, you came up with a
very, very good solution.

00:30:43.300 --> 00:30:44.360
So, why don't you
go and explain?

00:30:44.360 --> 00:30:46.484
MARK MANDEL: I didn't
personally come up with this.

00:30:46.484 --> 00:30:47.500
Engineers at Google did.

00:30:47.500 --> 00:30:49.480
FRANCESC CAMPOY: You came up
with the name of the solution,

00:30:49.480 --> 00:30:49.979
I guess.

00:30:49.979 --> 00:30:50.590
[CHUCKLES]

00:30:50.590 --> 00:30:52.660
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, so,
there is this really--

00:30:52.660 --> 00:30:55.360
I think, it's a neat sort of
thing called the Cloud SQL

00:30:55.360 --> 00:30:56.320
proxy.

00:30:56.320 --> 00:30:58.360
It works both for
MySQL and Postgres.

00:30:58.360 --> 00:31:02.600
Basically, it is an agent that
you can run on your machine.

00:31:02.600 --> 00:31:04.110
So either, in this
instance, we're

00:31:04.110 --> 00:31:06.190
talking about Compute
Engine, but you

00:31:06.190 --> 00:31:08.410
could run on Container
Engine, Docker instances, even

00:31:08.410 --> 00:31:10.100
locally on your client as well.

00:31:10.100 --> 00:31:11.740
So, it's an agent you install.

00:31:11.740 --> 00:31:13.180
You provide it
some credentials--

00:31:13.180 --> 00:31:15.490
so basically, service
keys, much like you

00:31:15.490 --> 00:31:17.560
would do with just
about anything else.

00:31:17.560 --> 00:31:20.072
And you can do that through
local environments or files,

00:31:20.072 --> 00:31:21.280
all sorts of different stuff.

00:31:21.280 --> 00:31:22.780
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And I'm
pretty sure it also even works

00:31:22.780 --> 00:31:25.034
with default credentials,
so if you're running on GCE,

00:31:25.034 --> 00:31:26.200
it just automatically works.

00:31:26.200 --> 00:31:28.450
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, so there's
lots of nice ways of doing it.

00:31:28.450 --> 00:31:30.130
And then, so you run
this agent locally,

00:31:30.130 --> 00:31:34.300
and its job is to
securely, over SSL, proxy

00:31:34.300 --> 00:31:36.670
the local connection,
you have for, say, MySQL,

00:31:36.670 --> 00:31:38.170
from the local
machine or that agent

00:31:38.170 --> 00:31:42.002
specifically, over to
the Cloud SQL instance.

00:31:42.002 --> 00:31:43.960
So this means then if
you're running this proxy

00:31:43.960 --> 00:31:45.910
and it has the right credentials
set up, and everything's great,

00:31:45.910 --> 00:31:48.910
and you're running it locally,
you don't need to do IP ranges.

00:31:48.910 --> 00:31:51.760
You just connect to
your Cloud SQL proxy,

00:31:51.760 --> 00:31:54.830
and it handles that whole
security thing for you.

00:31:54.830 --> 00:31:56.972
It's actually pretty
easy to set up.

00:31:56.972 --> 00:31:58.930
It's just a little agent
you can set up and run

00:31:58.930 --> 00:31:59.950
if you want to run it.

00:31:59.950 --> 00:32:02.110
There are also a
series of walkthroughs

00:32:02.110 --> 00:32:04.540
that are available in the
documentation that show you

00:32:04.540 --> 00:32:06.057
how to do it from
Compute Engine,

00:32:06.057 --> 00:32:08.390
from Container Engine, local
client, that kind of stuff,

00:32:08.390 --> 00:32:08.500
too.

00:32:08.500 --> 00:32:09.460
So, it's pretty great.

00:32:09.460 --> 00:32:11.240
And most of all, it's
also open source.

00:32:11.240 --> 00:32:12.630
You can go and have a
look at how it all works.

00:32:12.630 --> 00:32:14.560
It's on GitHub, and you
can check it out too.

00:32:14.560 --> 00:32:15.190
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:32:15.190 --> 00:32:15.690
Cool.

